s a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176024 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1045 "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176145 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "eb431efc-453b-5866-adc7-559d3c697e3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176231 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,4-x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac600244-dbff-596b-9fbb-094ec0801e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176231 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,Q(x,y)=4-x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176485 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdd86cce-9e5b-5a18-9af7-54a7f6efd03e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176485 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/45/459a51f3c408a9c647b3be6b978267a6ce724e747118fbe897275c370d2bb6d6.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1061 33843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "efebb741-9ea8-56f3-9f79-7c340d1fe301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176806 #edb/ref 1061 35775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176806 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "736cedc4-b5c1-5e97-b975-c6db4fd154c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176806 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1061 35776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b3de726-ead0-5369-a1fe-3dcc61b38bbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1061 35777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f69ccccf-5d34-5f08-95d0-7af5d0c7628b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\left( e^{-x^2}- x^2y \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(y+xy^2\\right) \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the unit circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise. \n\nDirect evaluation of this integral is likely to be a difficult task. However, we can make light work of this tricky integral using Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ (shown below) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/dd/dd859c753a8a5445eafab5db4f1e796b48997e1abb1728accd03fe3825e595d6.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}- x^2y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe obtain the partial derivatives   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{-{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis double integral will be easier to evaluate if we switch to plane polar coordinates. \n\nThe region $D$ in $xy$-coordinates can be represented as the region $\\Delta$ in polar coordinates, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral by applying the change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1065 5838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b5a23c7-4ccf-5122-8b5f-98fbabb0e367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416073 #edb/ref 1080 "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416073 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416073 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05ff21cb-9e33-53f3-b627-a88a79716c30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416074 #edb/ref 1